INT. LUNAR AGRICULTURAL DOME - CAFETERIA - DAY CYCLE

A circular canteen under a clear shell. Earth hangs in the sky like a blue marble. Long tables of recycled plastic, trays of steaming something. A hum of pumps from beneath the floor.

DR. HALCYON PRYCE, 50s, lead agronomist, white coat over coveralls, sits at a central table stirring something green with a spork. Across from her, TEODORO FALCONE, 40s, mechanic, grease under his nails, eats a protein bar with no pleasure. CHIOMA UDOH, 30s, hydroponics tech, tablet propped against a water pouch, scrolls a spreadsheet.

HALCYON
Do you taste that?

TEODORO
It's lunch.

HALCYON
It's kale. Mostly.

TEODORO
It's mostly lunch.

CHIOMA
Cycle three yield is down four percent.

HALCYON
Down from what?

CHIOMA
From the last harvest.

HALCYON
That's not a number. That's a mood.

CHIOMA
Four percent is a number.

HALCYON
It depends on who's counting.

TEODORO
I'm counting calories. Mine are low.

HALCYON
Teodoro, you've been quiet this week.

TEODORO
I'm always quiet.

HALCYON
Quiet-quiet or worried-quiet?

TEODORO
Pump seven is making a sound.

HALCYON
What kind of sound?

TEODORO
A mean one.

CHIOMA
Mean how?

TEODORO
Like a dog that hasn't decided yet.

HALCYON
Have you filed a ticket?

TEODORO
I filed three. Maintenance said "scheduled."

HALCYON
When?

TEODORO
Spring.

HALCYON
It's October.

TEODORO
I'm aware.

A figure appears at the cafeteria entrance. NADEEM ROUSSEAU, 20s, intern, lanky, glasses sliding down his nose, balances a tray with the posture of someone who has never carried a tray. He scans the room and heads toward them.

NADEEM
Is this seat taken?

HALCYON
Sit.

NADEEM
Thank you, Doctor.

HALCYON
Halcyon. Dr. is for formal complaints.

NADEEM
Halcyon. Okay.

He sits. Opens his mouth. Closes it. Opens it again.

NADEEM (CONT'D)
Sorry. I'm new. Today's my second day.

TEODORO
You'll get used to the food.

NADEEM
Is it always this beige?

CHIOMA
Only on days that end in Y.

NADEEM
What do you do here?

CHIOMA
I keep the plants alive. Teodoro keeps the plants' water alive. Halcyon keeps all of us alive.

HALCYON
Aspirationally.

NADEEM
That's a lot of alive.

HALCYON
On a rock with no air, it's the only job.

NADEEM
I read about the bio-loop in school. Water, nutrients, light. Every molecule counted.

HALCYON
And?

NADEEM
It's more beautiful in a textbook.

HALCYON
It's less beautiful in a textbook. The textbook doesn't smell.

TEODORO
(muttering)
Pump seven.

NADEEM
Sorry?

CHIOMA
Don't. He'll tell you for an hour.

TEODORO
It's a good story.

CHIOMA
It's a pump.

TEODORO
It's a pump with a personality.

NADEEM
I like pumps.

TEODORO
(looking up)
Kid, you're hired.

HALCYON
He's already hired.

TEODORO
Then you're promoted.

NADEEM
To what?

TEODORO
To the person who gets to listen.

CHIOMA
Good luck.

COMMANDER BENEDIKT STONE, 60s, dome administrator, cane tapping, pauses at the end of their table with a tray of nothing but coffee.

BENEDIKT
Dr. Pryce. Mr. Falcone. Is this the new hire?

NADEEM
Yes, sir.

BENEDIKT
Welcome to the Moon. Try not to break it.

He moves on. Nadeem laughs, uncertain. He picks up his spork and tastes the green stuff. His face does something complicated.

NADEEM
That's really kale.

HALCYON
Told you.

They eat. Pumps hum below. Overhead, Earth turns slowly, blue and white and very far away.
